[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The Delay Line v3 is a modular video-feedback engine expressed as the two
smallest composable atoms — a read head (**Delay Tap**) and a record head
(**Delay Write**) — over a shared GPU tape (**Delay Core**). They stack as:

  source -> [Delay Tap] -> fx1 -> fx2 -> [Delay Write] -> output

This document is the authoritative specification. v0.2 revises the tape
addressing model: reads and writes are now addressed **absolutely** off a
per-channel per-frame counter `frame_index` (see CORE-ADVANCE / TAP-READ-SLOT),
so a Tap reads the same delay regardless of its position relative to any Write in
the stack and regardless of how many Tap/Write instances interleave in a frame.
This supersedes the as-built v0.1 shared-write-pointer model (branch
`delay-line-v3`, commit `dcb44c6`), in which the read position was derived from a
pointer the Write mutated mid-frame and was therefore order-dependent (and one
frame too long — the old CORE-DEPTH off-by-one).

Requirements whose behaviour changes under v0.2 are marked STATUS: Draft with the
code delta from `dcb44c6` called out in their RATIONALE or VERIFICATION;
requirements unaffected by the addressing change retain STATUS: Built. Where the
prose in `requirements.md` disagrees, this document wins; `requirements.md` has
not yet been reconciled to v0.2.

v0.3 further simplifies the **Write into a pure write head**. The record equation
is now a straight overwrite `tape[slot] = Send * input`: the Write never reads the
tape and the **Regen** parameter is removed. The old v0.1/v0.2 record math
`(1 - Send) * Regen * old + Send * input` (a fade pre-pass plus an additive write)
was a lineage artifact of the shipped unified `DLMd` plugin, in which a single node
did its own in-place feedback; split into a Tap + Write pair it double-expressed
the loop the Tap already owns. **All feedback and mixing now live in the Tap**
(`out = Dry * source + Wet * tape`); the loop gain around `source -> Tap -> FX ->
Write` is `Send * Wet`. The Write drops from 7 to 6 FFGL params and touches no host
blend state (a single overwrite pass, blend disabled). Requirements changed by v0.3
call out the delta from `dcb44c6` in their RATIONALE/VERIFICATION.

The design goal is the *minimum playable control surface*: every knob is either a
plain gain or a time value, each does exactly one thing, and no knob couples two
behaviours. The prior unified `DLMd` plugin and the intermediate over-built v3
(Thru<->Playback, Buffer Mix, Blend-mode selector, multi-tap, Regen) are
superseded.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
# Terminology
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
- **Tape / ring buffer** — the per-channel GPU texture array that stores recent
  frames; the Write records into it, the Tap reads from it.
- **Slot / layer** — one frame of storage in the tape (one array layer).
- **Frame counter (`frame_index`, `t`)** — a per-channel monotonic count of host
  frames since allocation. It advances by exactly one **once per host frame**,
  performed by the first plugin — Tap *or* Write — to touch the Core that frame
  (the barrier, CORE-BARRIER). Every slot address is a pure function of this
  single per-frame value, so addresses do not change as plugins draw within the
  frame — this is what makes the read order-independent.
- **Write slot** — `t mod buf_size`: the slot this frame's Write records into
  (the Core does no GL/record itself; the Write *plugin* records there).
- **Read slot / full delay** — `(t − loop_length) mod buf_size`: the slot the Tap
  reads. Its content is exactly **`loop_length` frames old** — the realized delay
  equals the requested `loop_length`, no off-by-one (the v0.1 `+1` is gone, see
  CORE-DEPTH). Because `1 ≤ loop_length ≤ buf_size − 1`, the read slot is always a
  **different layer from the write slot**, so this frame's Write cannot disturb
  the read no matter which is drawn first — order-independence replaces the old
  draw-order-protects-the-read trick.
- **One lap / slot lifetime** — `buf_size = loop_length + 1` frames: how long a
  recorded frame survives before its slot is overwritten. The Tap reads a slot at
  age `loop_length`, i.e. exactly one frame before it is overwritten; that
  one-frame margin is the **guard slot** (the `+1` in `buf_size`), which is what
  keeps the read slot off the write slot.
- **Loop** — a Tap and a Write set to the same Channel, with FX between them;
  video recirculates once every `loop_length` frames (the recirculation period
  now matches the knob).
- **Injection** — new source material entering the loop (Tap Dry, Write Send).
- **Feedback / sustain** — how much of the recirculating tail survives each lap.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
# Delay Core (shared tape)
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: CORE-CHANNELS
TITLE: Fixed channel count
COMPONENT: Core
STATEMENT: >>>
The Core SHALL expose exactly 2 independent channels, indexed 0 and 1 and
presented to the user as labels "1" and "2". The count is the compile-time
constant `NUM_CHANNELS` and is not user-configurable at runtime.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Increasing the channel count (4–8) is VRAM-heavy and explicitly deferred to
fast-follow F6. Keeping it a constant (not a baked-in literal spread through the
code) makes that later bump a one-line change.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
Inspect `plugins/delay-core/src/lib.rs`: `NUM_CHANNELS == 2`. In Resolume, the
Channel selector on both plugins offers exactly two options, "1" and "2".
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: CORE-DEPTH
TITLE: Tape depth and usable delay range
COMPONENT: Core
STATEMENT: >>>
Each channel's tape SHALL hold `BUFFER_DEPTH` = 240 frame slots (the texture
array is always allocated at full depth, see CORE-FORMAT). The user sets a delay
parameter `loop_length` where `1 <= loop_length <= BUFFER_DEPTH - 1` (i.e.
1..=239); the ring **uses** `buf_size = loop_length + 1` of the 240 layers. The
**realized delay** — the age of the content the Tap reads — SHALL equal
`loop_length` frames exactly. The write slot (`t mod buf_size`) and the read slot
(`(t − loop_length) mod buf_size`) differ by `loop_length` (mod `buf_size`),
which is non-zero for every legal `loop_length`, so they are always distinct
layers. The `+1` in `buf_size` is a **size margin** — the **guard slot** — that
keeps them distinct: it makes `buf_size` one larger than `loop_length` so the
age-`loop_length` read address always trails the write address by a non-zero
offset. It is NOT a reserved/unused layer: with monotonic addressing the write
cycles through every one of the `buf_size` layers over `buf_size` frames, so all
of them hold live delayed content in rotation.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
v0.2 RESOLVES the v0.1 off-by-one. In v0.1 the read slot was `(wp + 1) mod
buf_size` computed from the live pointer, which was *identically* the slot the
Write advanced into and overwrote that frame; the read survived only because the
Tap was drawn first (COMPOSE-ORDER), and the realized delay came out at
`loop_length + 1`. v0.2 addresses reads and writes absolutely off the per-frame
counter `frame_index` (CORE-ADVANCE): the read targets
`(t − loop_length) mod buf_size`, a layer the current frame's Write never
touches, so (a) the delay is `loop_length` exactly and (b) the read is
independent of draw order. The `+1` extra layer is no longer dead weight — it is
the guard slot required for the full-delay read (age `loop_length`) to sit one
layer off the write target; at `loop_length = 239`, `buf_size = 240` uses all
240 layers, with the read exactly one layer behind the write.
CODE DELTA from `dcb44c6`: the Core must expose a monotonic `frame_index` and
compute slots as above, instead of returning a wrapping `write_pos` that both
sides offset by `+1`; see CORE-ADVANCE, CORE-ABI, TAP-READ-SLOT.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`BUFFER_DEPTH == 240`; the ring modulus is `buf_size = loop_length + 1` and
`loop_length` is clamped to `1..=BUFFER_DEPTH-1`. The allocation itself is always
240 layers (`alloc_buffer`), not `buf_size` layers. Realized delay is verifiable
live: set Frames mode to N, Send=1, feed a single flash frame (after warm-up so
the referenced slot has been written), and count host frames until the Tap
reproduces it — the count SHALL be exactly N (no seed-lap off-by-one, because
both slots derive from the same `frame_index`). During the first `loop_length`
frames after allocation the read address lands on a slot not yet written this lap;
because every layer is cleared to black on allocation (CORE-FORMAT), the read
returns black (no spurious echo) — this warm-up is handled by the cleared buffer,
NOT by the Wet-mute in TAP-EMPTY-BUFFER (which fires only when the tape is
unallocated).
<<<
STATUS: Draft

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: CORE-FORMAT
TITLE: Tape pixel format
COMPONENT: Core
STATEMENT: >>>
The tape SHALL be an RGBA16F (16-bit half-float per channel) 2D texture array.
Float storage removes the 8-bit banding that the linear-blend feedback tail
otherwise shows (TAP-BLEND-SPACE) and can hold values with headroom. Over-unity
(>1.0) accumulation is still NOT retained across laps, because the feedback loop
passes through Resolume's inter-effect FBO (8-bit unless the host composits in
float, which is outside our control) and the Write overwrites rather than
accumulates — so the tape receives already-clipped [0,1] input each lap.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This lands the precision half of the old DEFER-FLOAT (F3). The realizable win is
banding removal, not the over-unity runaway the original note imagined — that
needs a float loop path (host float mode) and/or additive tape accumulation
(DEFER-IFS), both still deferred. VRAM is kept affordable by storing the tape at
reduced resolution (WRITE-TAPE-SCALE): at 0.5× the RGBA16F tape (8 bytes/texel) is
~0.97 GB/channel at 1080p×240 — less than the old full-res RGBA8 (~1.94 GB). The
Crisp<->Deep budget is thus partially realized (a fixed spatial scale); trading
resolution for a deeper buffer / longer max delay (dynamic `BUFFER_DEPTH`) remains
deferred.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`alloc_buffer` creates a `GL_RGBA16F` `GL_TEXTURE_2D_ARRAY` (`GL_HALF_FLOAT`) with
`BUFFER_DEPTH` layers, cleared to zero via `clear_texture_array`. Tier-2
headless-GL still passes (8-bit-in values round-trip exactly through half-float),
incl. scenario 8 which reads a half-res tape back at full res.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: CORE-ADVANCE
TITLE: Frame counter — one advance per host frame
COMPONENT: Core
STATEMENT: >>>
The Core SHALL maintain a per-channel monotonic frame counter `frame_index` and
advance it by exactly one per host frame. The advance is performed once per frame
by the first plugin to touch the Core that frame — Tap *or* Write (see
CORE-BARRIER) — regardless of how many plugin instances read or write the channel
that frame. The write slot is `frame_index mod buf_size` and the full-delay read
slot is `(frame_index − loop_length) mod buf_size`; both are pure functions of
`frame_index`, so every plugin in the frame computes the same addresses no matter
the draw order or how many Tap/Write instances interleave.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Advancing a monotonic counter (not a wrapping write pointer) and addressing slots
absolutely off it is what makes the Tap read order-independent (TAP-READ-SLOT)
and removes the CORE-DEPTH off-by-one. In v0.1 the advance was owned by the Write
and happened mid-frame, so a Tap drawn before a Write saw a different pointer
value than one drawn after; here the single advance is frame-scoped and every
address derives from it, so the read cannot shift within a frame.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`dc_frame_tick` advances `frame_index` only inside the `first_touch` branch;
subsequent callers with the same `frame_id` leave it unchanged. Observable: a
flash fed at frame k reproduces at exactly frame k + loop_length, and a
Tap→Write→Tap sequence on one Channel makes both Taps read identical content
(v0.1 made them read one-frame-different content).
<<<
STATUS: Draft

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: CORE-BARRIER
TITLE: Per-frame barrier keyed on host frame id
COMPONENT: Core
STATEMENT: >>>
The Core SHALL identify a host frame by a host-provided per-frame id (the FFGL
`host_time` truncated to whole milliseconds). The first `dc_frame_tick` call for
a channel with a new `frame_id` is the "first touch": it advances `frame_index`
and returns 1. Later calls in the same `frame_id` return 0 and do not advance.
Both plugins call `dc_frame_tick` at the top of their draw, so whichever of them
(Tap or Write) draws first advances the frame. A freshly (re)allocated buffer
resets the barrier so the next touch is treated as first-of-frame.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Keying on host frame id (not 2 ms wall-clock) makes the advance deterministic
under variable render load. In v0.2 the barrier is touched by the Tap as well as
the Write, so the frame counter is frame-scoped rather than Write-scoped — this
is what lets a Tap drawn before the Write compute the same address as one drawn
after (CORE-ADVANCE); the Tap advances the clock only when first-of-frame and
still never writes tape pixels (TAP-READ-ONLY). NOTE (degenerate, unsupported): a
frame in which a channel is touched only by Tap(s) and no Write still advances
the counter, so a Tap with no matching Write reads progressively older slots
(rotates the tape) rather than freezing. Like the multi-writer case
(COMPOSE-SINGLE-WRITER) this is called out rather than guarded. Deterministic
multi-writer accumulation remains the deferred additive-IFS refinement (F5).
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`dc_frame_tick` computes `first_touch = !ch.frame_active || ch.frame_id !=
frame_id`. NOTE (open): if the host ever reports the same whole-millisecond
`host_time` for two consecutive frames (>=1000 fps, or a stalled clock), the
second frame's touch is mis-classified as same-frame and the counter does not
advance. Confirm host_time resolution is adequate on target hardware.
<<<
STATUS: Draft

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: CORE-REALLOC
TITLE: Reallocation on resolution change
COMPONENT: Core
STATEMENT: >>>
When the incoming frame width or height differs from the currently allocated
tape, the Core SHALL reallocate that channel's tape to the new size. The old
tape contents are discarded (the loop is reseeded from empty).
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`needs_alloc` triggers when `b.width != width || b.height != height`; the old
texture is deleted and a fresh cleared array allocated.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: CORE-LENGTH-RESEED
TITLE: Reseed the tape to black on a loop-length change
COMPONENT: Core
STATEMENT: >>>
When the effective `loop_length` changes without a resolution change, the Core
SHALL clear the whole tape (all `BUFFER_DEPTH` layers) to black before adopting
the new length. The read then warms up from black over one lap, identically to a
fresh allocation (CORE-DEPTH), rather than reading residual content.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
`loop_length` IS the ring modulus (`buf_size = loop_length + 1`), and every slot
address is `frame_index mod buf_size` (CORE-ADVANCE). Changing the modulus re-maps
which frame each layer represents, so ALL previously-recorded layers become
temporally incoherent — not just a tail. The worst symptom is *expansion*: e.g.
record at loop_length 60, shrink to 10 (only ~10 layers get rewritten), then expand
back to 60 — the read now sweeps ~50 layers the short loop never rewrote and
resurfaces stale pre-shrink frames for up to one lap. A full reseed makes the
behaviour a clean warm-up in every case (shrink or expand). Because `loop_length`
is latched (WRITE-TIME-LATCH holds it steady frame-to-frame), this fires only on a
real change, not per frame; NOTE: continuously *dragging* the Frames/Ms value
re-clears each step (the tape stays black while dragging) — smooth time change is
DEFER-SWEEP, and beat-sync / discrete values reseed once as intended.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`dc_frame_tick`'s retune branch compares the new clamped `loop_length` to the
stored one and, on a difference, calls `clear_texture_array(tex)` (an FBO +
`glClear` per layer — GPU-side, no multi-GB CPU upload; the same helper
`alloc_buffer` uses) before updating `buf.loop_length`. Tier-2 headless-GL
(`delay-gl-tests` scenario 7): fill a loop_length-8 ring so every slot is white,
change the length, and a previously-white layer reads back black.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: CORE-REFCOUNT
TITLE: Channel acquire/release refcounting
COMPONENT: Core
STATEMENT: >>>
Each plugin instance SHALL acquire the channel it uses (`dc_acquire`) and
release it on drop or channel change (`dc_release`), keeping a per-channel
reference count. `dc_release` SHALL perform no GL calls, because it can run off
the GL thread; the tape texture name is allowed to leak until process exit.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Matches the shipped v2 plugin's safe-teardown behaviour. The only GL delete is in
the realloc path of `dc_frame_tick`, which always runs on the GL thread.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`dc_release` contains no `gl::` calls; both plugins call `acquire`/`release` in
`new`, `set_param` (on Channel change), and `Drop`.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: CORE-ABI
TITLE: Cross-DLL C ABI
COMPONENT: Core
STATEMENT: >>>
The Core SHALL be a single C-ABI cdylib (`delay_core.dll`) exposing `dc_*`
functions, runtime-loaded by each plugin DLL from its own directory. The GPU
texture handles it owns are shared across the plugin DLLs via the shared GL
context; only texture handles cross the DLL boundary, not FBOs.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`delay_core` exports `dc_buffer_depth`, `dc_acquire`, `dc_release`,
`dc_frame_tick`, `dc_tex`, `dc_frame_index`, `dc_buf_size`. v0.2 renames
`dc_begin_frame_write` → `dc_frame_tick` (now called by both plugins, CORE-
BARRIER) and replaces the wrapping `dc_write_pos` with the monotonic
`dc_frame_index`. Plugins reach them through `pluglib::api()`.
<<<
STATUS: Draft

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
# Delay Write (DlyW) — record head
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: WRITE-IDENTITY
TITLE: Plugin identity
COMPONENT: Write
STATEMENT: >>>
The Write plugin SHALL register with unique id `DlyW` and the exactly-16-byte
name `"Delay Write     "`, as an FFGL Effect.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`plugin_info()` returns `unique_id: b"DlyW"`, `name: b"Delay Write     "` (16
bytes), `ty: Effect`.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: WRITE-RECORD-MATH
TITLE: Record equation
COMPONENT: Write
STATEMENT: >>>
Each frame the Write SHALL record its input into the current write slot as a
straight overwrite:

  tape[slot] = Send * input

where `input` is the current frame. The Write is a **pure write head**: it never
reads the slot's prior content. This is implemented as a single shader pass with
GL blend DISABLED, so the pass fully replaces the slot's pixels; there is no fade
pre-pass and no additive blend.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
v0.3 removes the old `(1 - Send) * Regen * old` decay term (and with it the
`Regen` parameter, see the removal noted in SYS-PARAM-COUNTS). That term was a
lineage artifact of the shipped unified `DLMd` plugin, where a single node did its
own in-place feedback; in a Tap + Write pair it double-expressed the loop the Tap
already owns (`out = Dry*source + Wet*tape`), so the same recirculating tail was
fed back twice — once by the Write's `Regen*old` and once by the Tap's `Wet`. With
the decay term gone, **all feedback and mixing live in the Tap** and the Write is a
transparent recorder: it just commits `Send` of the current frame into the tape.
The whole-loop feedback coefficient collapses from `(1-Send)*Regen + Send*Wet` to
simply `Send * Wet` (see LOOP-FEEDBACK-COEFF). Removing the buffer read also means
the Write touches no GL blend state (COMPOSE-GLSTATE), shrinking the state-restore
surface. CODE DELTA from `dcb44c6`: the fade pre-pass and the additive
`CONSTANT_COLOR=Send`, `ONE` blend are deleted; `fade.frag.glsl` is removed; the
record is a single `write_pass` with blend disabled. The stale comments the v0.2
draft catalogued here (lib.rs module doc, fade.frag.glsl header, shader.rs
terminology, the delay-core "spare slot" comment) have been reconciled to this
model in code.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`draw_write` (`delay-write/src/lib.rs`) records into
`write_slot = frame_index mod buf_size` (`delay_dsp::Ring::write_slot`) via a
single `shaders.write_pass(input_tex, uv_scale, send)` with `gl::BLEND` disabled;
`write.frag.glsl` computes `out_color = u_send * texture(u_input, ...)`. There is
no `fade_pass` and no `regen` term anywhere in the crate. With Send=1 the slot
becomes `input` exactly; with Send=0 the slot becomes black (over one lap the whole
tape clears).
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: WRITE-PASSTHROUGH
TITLE: Transparent output
COMPONENT: Write
STATEMENT: >>>
The Write's video output SHALL be a pixel-for-pixel passthrough of its input
(dry=1, wet=0). The record head is visually transparent — the user sees the
frame being recorded, never the tape contents. A Write with no matching Tap
records silently ("into the aether").
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
At 100% host effect opacity (see COMPOSE-OPACITY) a passthrough output means the
Write neither adds nor removes anything visible; the loop is monitored solely by
a Tap on the same Channel.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
After the record passes, `draw_write` binds the host FBO and draws
`output.draw(..., dry=1.0, wet=0.0)` with the input texture. Output equals input.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: WRITE-PARAM-CHANNEL
TITLE: Channel parameter
COMPONENT: Write
STATEMENT: >>>
Param 0 "Channel" SHALL be an Option with values {"1","2"} (default "1"). It
selects which shared tape this Write records into; a Tap on the same Channel
forms a loop. Changing it releases the old channel's refcount and acquires the
new one.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`PARAM_CHANNEL` Option, default 0.0; `set_param` re-acquires on change;
`channel()` maps <0.5 -> 0 else 1.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: WRITE-PARAM-TIME
TITLE: Time (delay length) parameters
COMPONENT: Write
STATEMENT: >>>
The tape length SHALL be set by a "Sync Mode" Option {Subdivision, Ms, Frames}
plus the value field for the active mode:

- Subdivision (default): Option of {1/16, 1/8, 1/4, 1/2, 1 bar, 2 bars, 4 bars} in beats {0.25,0.5,1,2,4,8,16}; default 1/4. Frames = `round(beats * (60/BPM) * fps)`, where BPM is host tempo and `fps` is the estimated frame rate (WRITE-FPS-ESTIMATE). If host BPM <= 0 the delay defaults to 30 frames (the chosen subdivision is ignored).
- Ms: Integer 1..4000 ms (default 500). Frames = `round((ms/1000) * fps)`.
- Frames: Integer 1..239 (default 30), used directly.

The computed frame count is clamped to `1..=240` inside the Write
(`compute_delay_frames`); the Core then clamps the effective loop length to
`1..=239` (CORE-DEPTH). Consequently a request of exactly 240 frames (e.g. Ms=4000
at 60 fps) latches/reports 240 in the Write while the loop physically runs at 239.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Conceptually one control ("Time"), but exposed as a mode selector plus per-mode
value fields because FFGL params are flat (see SYS-PARAM-COUNTS). Sweepable/
continuous time is deferred (F2); Time here is latched (see WRITE-TIME-LATCH).
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
Params 1–4 are Sync Mode (Option), Subdivision (Option, encoded `i/6.0`),
Delay Ms (Integer 1..4000), Delay Frames (Integer 1..239). `compute_delay_frames`
(`delay-write/src/lib.rs:59-76`) implements the three conversions with `.round()`
and `d.clamp(1, max)` where `max = dc_buffer_depth() = 240`; the 239 clamp is
applied downstream in `dc_frame_tick`. The BPM<=0 branch returns
`30.min(max)`.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: WRITE-TIME-LATCH
TITLE: Latched delay recomputation
COMPONENT: Write
STATEMENT: >>>
The delay length in frames SHALL be recomputed only when an input to the
computation actually changes: sync mode, subdivision, ms, frames, a BPM change
> 0.5, or the first frame. Between changes it is held (latched).
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Avoids per-frame jitter in the tape length from noisy BPM/FPS estimates.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`delay_frames` recomputes only when `changed`; otherwise returns
`latched_delay`.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: WRITE-FPS-ESTIMATE
TITLE: FPS estimation for time conversion
COMPONENT: Write
STATEMENT: >>>
The Write SHALL estimate frame rate with an exponential moving average (alpha
0.05) of instantaneous 1/delta, ignoring deltas <= 0 or >= 1 s, starting from a
60 fps seed. This estimate converts Ms and Subdivision delays to frames.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`update_fps` implements the EMA; `fps_estimate` starts at 60.0.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: WRITE-PARAM-SEND
TITLE: Send parameter
COMPONENT: Write
STATEMENT: >>>
Param 5 "Send" SHALL be a Standard [0,1] control (default 1) with a linear
response. It is the dub throw: how hard the current frame commits into the loop
(`tape = Send * input`). At Send=1 the slot is replaced by the input; pulsing
Send down (with Tap Wet high) throws intermittent new material while the existing
tail keeps recirculating through the Tap. At Send=0 nothing new is recorded and
the tape clears to black over one lap.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Linear so it automates predictably when pulsed/modulated. v0.3 removed the Regen
parameter (see WRITE-RECORD-MATH); Send is now the Write's only gain, and loop
sustain is set entirely by the Tap's Wet (LOOP-FEEDBACK-COEFF). CODE DELTA from
`dcb44c6`: Send moved from param index 6 to 5.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`send()` returns the raw value; default 1.0; drives the single `write_pass`
(`tape = send * input`, blend disabled). No fade/additive-blend coefficient
remains.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: WRITE-BUFFER-FAIL
TITLE: Graceful passthrough on allocation failure
COMPONENT: Write
STATEMENT: >>>
If the tape texture is unavailable or has zero size, the Write SHALL still pass
its input through untouched and perform no record.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`draw_write` early-returns with a passthrough `output.draw(dry=1,wet=0)` when
`tex == 0 || buf_size == 0`.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: WRITE-TAPE-SCALE
TITLE: Reduced-resolution tape storage
COMPONENT: Write
STATEMENT: >>>
The Write SHALL size the tape at `TAPE_SCALE` × the incoming frame resolution
(TAPE_SCALE = 0.5, i.e. half width and height = a quarter of the pixels) and
record into that reduced-resolution layer. The Write's own video output stays
full-resolution (WRITE-PASSTHROUGH); only the STORED loop is downscaled. The Tap
reads the tape with normalised UVs and linear filtering (TAP-READ-MATH,
`texture(u_buffer, vec3(v_uv, layer))`), so it upscales on read with no Tap-side
change and no knowledge of the tape resolution.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A quarter of the pixels makes the RGBA16F float tape (CORE-FORMAT, 8 bytes/texel)
cost about half of the old full-res RGBA8 one, so float precision is affordable at
1080p — the chosen Crisp<->Deep operating point. It is also a creative control:
because only the recirculating (Wet) path is re-downsampled each lap while the Dry
injection stays full-res, the feedback tail softens progressively (classic
feedback blur) while freshly-injected material stays crisp. The single fixed scale
avoids adding a param; a live Full/Half/Quarter control is a possible follow-up.
Downsample is a single bilinear tap, adequate at exactly 0.5× (mild aliasing on
very fine input detail is masked by the feedback softening; a 2×2 box tap is an
easy refinement if it matters).
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`delay-write/src/lib.rs`: `TAPE_SCALE == 0.5`; `draw_write` computes
`tape_w/tape_h = round(TAPE_SCALE * width/height)` (min 1), passes them to
`dc_frame_tick` (so the Core allocates the tape at those dims), and sets the write
pass viewport to `tape_w × tape_h` while the passthrough output uses the full host
viewport. `uv_scale` still targets the full-res input, so the write pass is a
full-frame downsample into the smaller layer.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
# Delay Tap (DlyT) — read head
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TAP-IDENTITY
TITLE: Plugin identity
COMPONENT: Tap
STATEMENT: >>>
The Tap plugin SHALL register with unique id `DlyT` and the exactly-16-byte name
`"Delay Tap       "`, as an FFGL Effect.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`plugin_info()` returns `unique_id: b"DlyT"`, `name: b"Delay Tap       "` (16
bytes), `ty: Effect`.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TAP-READ-MATH
TITLE: Read equation
COMPONENT: Tap
STATEMENT: >>>
The Tap output SHALL be:

  out = clamp(Dry * source  +  Wet * tape[full-delay], 0, 1)

where `source` is the live input and `tape[full-delay]` is the oldest tape slot.
Dry and Wet are two independent gains, NOT a crossfade: they can be set freely
and independently (e.g. full tail AND full new material simultaneously). The
equation above is the **Perceptual** form; the colour space this sum is computed
in is selected by the Tap's Blend Space param (TAP-BLEND-SPACE, **default
Linear**) — in Linear mode `source` and the tape are decoded to ~linear light
before the sum and the clamped result is re-encoded.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Two independent gains are the decoupling insight of the whole redesign: a single
blend knob always couples "how much source enters" with "how much loop
sustains".
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`output.frag.glsl` computes `clamp(u_dry*live + u_wet*buf, 0, 1)`; `draw_tap`
passes `dry = Dry`, `wet = Wet`.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TAP-READ-SLOT
TITLE: Fixed full-delay read position
COMPONENT: Tap
STATEMENT: >>>
The Tap SHALL read slot `(frame_index − loop_length) mod buf_size`, which holds
content exactly `loop_length` frames old — the requested delay, with no
off-by-one (see CORE-DEPTH). Because the address derives only from the per-frame
`frame_index` (CORE-ADVANCE) and lands on a different layer from the write slot,
a Tap reads the same content regardless of its position relative to any Write,
and regardless of how many Tap/Write instances interleave in the frame. The read
position is not user-adjustable in this version.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
v0.2 decouples the read from draw order. In v0.1 the read slot was the live
`(wp + 1)` — *identically* the slot the Write overwrote that frame — so a Tap
read correctly only because it was drawn first, and a Tap→Write→Tap sequence made
the two Taps read one-frame-different content. Addressing off the frame-scoped
`frame_index` and landing on a layer the Write never touches removes both
problems. Placement now affects only signal routing (whether FX re-process the
tail, COMPOSE-ORDER), never the delay length. Variable/multi-tap is deferred (F1).
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`draw_tap` sets `read_pos = (frame_index + buf_size − loop_length) % buf_size`
(v0.2; was `(wp + 1) % buf_size` off the pre-advance pointer). Realized age is
`loop_length` frames (CORE-DEPTH); two Taps bracketing a Write on the same
Channel read identical content.
<<<
STATUS: Draft

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TAP-READ-ONLY
TITLE: Never mutates tape contents; only advances the frame clock
COMPONENT: Tap
STATEMENT: >>>
The Tap SHALL never write tape pixels; it only reads. It MAY advance the frame
counter: in v0.2 the Tap calls `dc_frame_tick` at the top of its draw
(CORE-BARRIER) and, if it is first-of-frame, advances `frame_index` — but it runs
no fade/record pass and never mutates tape *contents*.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The Tap must participate in the barrier so the frame counter is frame-scoped, not
Write-scoped (CORE-ADVANCE); advancing a counter is not the same as writing tape
data, so the read-only-*contents* guarantee still holds.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`draw_tap` calls `dc_frame_tick` (barrier only), `tex`, `frame_index`,
`buf_size` — no fade/record pass, no GL write into the tape.
<<<
STATUS: Draft

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TAP-EMPTY-BUFFER
TITLE: Wet muted before the tape exists
COMPONENT: Tap
STATEMENT: >>>
If the tape is unallocated or zero-size, the Tap SHALL force Wet to 0 so only
`Dry * source` passes; the source is never lost waiting for a buffer.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Removes the seeding chicken-and-egg: the loop fills from the source on lap one
because the Tap always carries the source (Dry) forward.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`draw_tap` sets `(read_pos, wet) = (0, 0.0)` when `buf_size == 0 || tex == 0`.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TAP-PARAM-CHANNEL
TITLE: Channel parameter
COMPONENT: Tap
STATEMENT: >>>
Param 0 "Channel" SHALL be an Option {"1","2"} (default "1") selecting the source
tape; a Write on the same Channel forms a loop. Changing it re-refcounts as in
WRITE-PARAM-CHANNEL.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`PARAM_CHANNEL` Option default 0.0; `set_param` re-acquires on change.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TAP-PARAM-DRY
TITLE: Dry parameter
COMPONENT: Tap
STATEMENT: >>>
Param 1 "Dry" SHALL be a Standard [0,1] linear gain (default 1) on the live
source into the FX chain. Dry is injection only: it never sets the tail. Dry=0
means no new material enters and the loop rings out per its feedback.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`dry()` returns raw value; default 1.0; used as `u_dry`.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TAP-PARAM-WET
TITLE: Wet parameter
COMPONENT: Tap
STATEMENT: >>>
Param 2 "Wet" SHALL be a Standard [0,1] linear gain (default 0.5) on the delayed
tape read. Wet is the loop feedback that downstream FX re-process each lap. At
full Send, Wet=1 is an infinite echo and Wet<1 decays.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`wet()` returns raw value; default 0.5; used as `u_wet` (subject to
TAP-EMPTY-BUFFER).
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: TAP-BLEND-SPACE
TITLE: Blend Space parameter (perceptual vs linear)
COMPONENT: Tap
STATEMENT: >>>
Param 3 "Blend Space" SHALL be an Option {"Perceptual","Linear"} (default
"Linear") selecting the colour space of the Tap's Dry/Wet sum (TAP-READ-MATH):

- Perceptual: `out = clamp(Dry*live + Wet*tape, 0, 1)` on the sRGB-encoded values
  directly. This is the exact legacy behaviour; the per-lap `×Wet` reads as a
  perceptually-even fade.
- Linear: `out = encode(clamp(Dry*decode(live) + Wet*decode(tape), 0, 1))` where
  `decode(c) = c^g`, `encode(c) = c^(1/g)`, and `g` = 2.2 (a cheap ~sRGB
  stand-in). The sum and the clamp happen in ~linear light — cleaner colour mixing
  and additive highlight bloom.

Only the RGB is mapped; alpha blends linearly in both modes. The tape itself is
unchanged (the Write is still a straight sRGB store, WRITE-RECORD-MATH); the Tap
decodes the tape on read and the loop re-encodes each lap, so in Linear mode the
effective loop-feedback coefficient (LOOP-FEEDBACK-COEFF) is `Wet·Send^g` rather
than `Wet·Send` — a monotonic reshaping of the Send curve, still < 1 for stability.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Now that the model is simplified to a single blend point (Regen removed,
WRITE-RECORD-MATH), the linear-vs-perceptual choice is a clean, isolated knob: one
shader branch in the shared output pass plus one Tap param, no Core/ABI/Write
change. Exposed as a toggle so it can be A/B'd live rather than baked in. The
`g=2.2` power curve matches the project preference for simple math over an exact
sRGB piecewise transfer; the exact transfer (and the RGBA16F storage that would
stop the 8-bit sRGB round-trip from banding over deep feedback) ride with
DEFER-FLOAT.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`delay-tap/src/params.rs`: `PARAM_BLEND == 3`, Option default 1.0 (Linear);
`gamma()` returns 1.0 (Perceptual) or `LINEAR_GAMMA == 2.2` (Linear), passed as `u_gamma` to
the shared output pass. `output.frag.glsl` takes the exact legacy path when
`u_gamma == 1.0` and the decode→blend→encode path otherwise. Tier-2 headless-GL
(`delay-gl-tests` scenario 6): a tape holding 200 with Dry=0, Wet=0.5 reads back
≈146 in Linear (`255·(0.5·(200/255)^2.2)^(1/2.2)`) vs 100 in Perceptual.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
# Composition, feedback semantics, and usage
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LOOP-FEEDBACK-COEFF
TITLE: Total loop-feedback coefficient
COMPONENT: Composition
STATEMENT: >>>
For a Tap and Write on the same Channel with FX of unity gain between them, the
per-lap feedback coefficient of the loop SHALL be:

  feedback = Send * Wet

feedback >= 1 sustains or blooms; feedback < 1 fades. Injection into the loop is
`Send * Dry`-scaled source (Write Send and Tap Dry), independent of the tail.
(With the Tap's Blend Space = Linear, TAP-BLEND-SPACE, the coefficient becomes
`Send^g * Wet`, g=2.2 — the same runaway/decay conclusions hold, Send is just
reshaped.)
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The single equation that lets a user reason about runaway vs. decay; a derived
property of WRITE-RECORD-MATH (`tape = Send*input`) and TAP-READ-MATH
(`out = Dry*source + Wet*tape`), stated here so it can be verified end-to-end, not
just per-atom. Each lap a signal is multiplied by `Wet` at the Tap read and by
`Send` at the Write record, so the round-trip gain is exactly `Send * Wet`. v0.3
made this exact: with Regen removed there is a single feedback path (through the
Tap), so the v0.2 "two adjacent-frame taps, `≈`" caveat is gone — the coefficient
is `=`, not `≈`. Loop sustain is now set entirely at the Tap's Wet (at full Send);
Send only scales how much recirculates and how much fresh material is committed.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
Live: Channel-matched Tap+Write, no FX, Dry=0. With Send=1, Wet=1 -> a still
image holds indefinitely (infinite echo). With Send=1, Wet=0.5 -> visible
halving each lap. With Send=0 -> the Write records black, so the tape (and the
loop) clears over one lap regardless of Wet.
<<<
STATUS: Draft

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: COMPOSE-OPACITY
TITLE: 100% opacity usage rule
COMPONENT: Composition
STATEMENT: >>>
All three effects SHALL be used at 100% host effect opacity; all mixing is done
in-shader. At 100% opacity the host's per-effect opacity crossfade is a no-op, so
the engine never depends on undefined host-opacity behaviour for FFGL effects.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Host FFGL opacity behaviour was never certain. WRITE-PASSTHROUGH and TAP-READ-
MATH (the Tap self-samples its input) together ensure the source survives without
leaning on host opacity — the fragility of the old Read model.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
Observable pass/fail: with a single Tap at Dry=1, Wet=0 at 100% opacity, the
output frame is identical to the input (bypass) — bit-identical at 1:1 texel
mapping (`uv_scale == 1`), otherwise identical within resampling tolerance where
the host texture is hardware-padded (`Width != HardwareWidth`). Verify by frame
diff. This
requirement is a *usage rule* the software does not self-enforce; it is
documented in the manual/release notes and checked by the frame-diff test above,
not by any runtime guard.
<<<
STATUS: Draft

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: COMPOSE-ORDER
TITLE: Tap-first stacking order
COMPONENT: Composition
STATEMENT: >>>
In a feedback stack the Tap SHALL be placed first (upstream of any FX and of the
Write): `source -> Tap -> fx... -> Write`. In v0.2 this is purely a *signal
routing* rule, not a timing one: Tap-first is what routes the recirculating tail
through the FX each lap, so downstream FX re-process the fed-back content. The
**delay length no longer depends on placement** — because the read address is a
pure function of the per-frame `frame_index` and lands on a layer the Write never
touches (TAP-READ-SLOT, CORE-ADVANCE), a Tap reads content `loop_length` frames
old wherever it sits; a Tap drawn after the Write reads the same delay as one
drawn before (it merely bypasses the between-Tap-and-Write FX for the fed-back
content). This is the v0.1→v0.2 change: v0.1 coupled delay length to draw order
(pre-advance read = `loop_length + 1`, post-advance = `loop_length`); v0.2
decouples timing from placement entirely.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
Usage rule, not self-enforced. Observable: place the Tap *after* the Write on the
same Channel and the delay is unchanged (`loop_length` either way, v0.2) but the
between-Tap-and-Write FX no longer re-process the tail. Consistent with
TAP-READ-SLOT.
<<<
STATUS: Draft

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: COMPOSE-GLSTATE
TITLE: Host GL state preservation
COMPONENT: System
STATEMENT: >>>
Each plugin SHALL save and restore the host GL state it disturbs around its draw:
the bound framebuffer, the viewport, the SCISSOR_TEST / BLEND / DEPTH_TEST
enables, and the active texture unit plus its `GL_TEXTURE0` binding. It shares the
GL context with Resolume.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
v0.3 RESOLVES the v0.2 open GL-state gap by shrinking the surface and widening the
restore. Because the Write is now a pure overwrite with blend DISABLED
(WRITE-RECORD-MATH), neither plugin mutates `BlendFunc` or `BlendColor` at all —
the old `CONSTANT_COLOR, ONE` additive blend is gone — so there is nothing there
to leak. Both plugins now also snapshot and restore the active texture unit and
the `GL_TEXTURE0` binding (bind during a pass, then put back the host's prior
texture, not 0), satisfying the project convention that the host's unit-0 texture
must never be left unbound. The remaining disturbed state (FBO, viewport, the
three enables) is snapshotted and restored exactly.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
Both `draw` methods (`delay-write/src/lib.rs`, `delay-tap/src/lib.rs`) snapshot
`FRAMEBUFFER_BINDING`, `VIEWPORT`, the SCISSOR/BLEND/DEPTH_TEST enables, and
`ACTIVE_TEXTURE` + `TEXTURE_BINDING_2D` on unit 0, and restore exactly those.
Neither calls `gl::BlendFunc` or `gl::BlendColor`.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-DEPLOY
TITLE: Co-located deployment of three DLLs
COMPONENT: System
STATEMENT: >>>
`delay_core.dll` SHALL be deployed beside both plugin DLLs (`DlyW`, `DlyT`),
because each plugin runtime-loads the core from its own directory. All three
ship together.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
Build/deploy places `delay_core.dll`, `delay-write.dll`, `delay-tap.dll` in the
same plugin directory; plugins load the core via `pluglib`.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-MIGRATION
TITLE: Migration from unified DLMd
COMPONENT: System
STATEMENT: >>>
Existing `DLMd` compositions have no automatic migration; they SHALL be rebuilt
onto the two-plugin Tap+Write model. This is a documented breaking change.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
Deployment guidance, not a software behaviour. Observable: there is no shared
save/preset format between `DLMd` and `DlyW`/`DlyT`, so an old composition loads
`DLMd` (if still installed) unchanged and must be re-patched by hand. Verified by
the absence of any `DLMd`->`DlyW/DlyT` conversion path in code or release tooling.
<<<
STATUS: Draft

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-PARAM-COUNTS
TITLE: Fixed FFGL parameter counts
COMPONENT: System
STATEMENT: >>>
The Write plugin SHALL expose exactly 6 FFGL parameters (indices 0..5: Channel,
Sync Mode, Subdivision, Delay Ms, Delay Frames, Send) and the Tap plugin exactly
4 (indices 0..3: Channel, Dry, Wet, Blend Space). The "minimum playable control
surface" / "two-atom" framing counts *conceptual* knobs — Channel, Time, Send on
the Write and Channel, Dry, Wet, Blend Space on the Tap — where the single
conceptual "Time" control is realised as 4 flat FFGL params (Sync Mode + the three
per-mode value fields) because FFGL parameters are a flat list with no grouping.
The four *level/time* knobs across a loop are Time, Send, Dry, Wet; Blend Space
(TAP-BLEND-SPACE) is a mode toggle, not a level.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Pins the exact host-visible surface so it can be checked, and explicitly
reconciles the prose "two-atom" language with the 6+3 flat param reality so a
reviewer is not left to guess whether the counts are a defect. CODE DELTA from
`dcb44c6`: v0.3 removed the `Regen` param (WRITE-RECORD-MATH), dropping the Write
from 7 params to 6 and re-indexing Send from 6 to 5.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`delay-write/src/params.rs`: `NUM_PARAMS == 6` (no `PARAM_REGEN`; `PARAM_SEND ==
5`); `delay-tap/src/params.rs`: `NUM_PARAMS == 4` (`PARAM_BLEND == 3`). Each
plugin's `num_params()` returns that constant.
<<<
STATUS: Built

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: COMPOSE-SINGLE-WRITER
TITLE: Single writer per channel
COMPONENT: Composition
STATEMENT: >>>
v3 SHALL be used with at most one Delay Write per Channel. Placing two or more
Writes on the same Channel is unsupported: each additional Write, seen by the
Core as a same-frame (non-first) touch, does not advance the frame counter but
still runs its own overwrite pass into the write slot
(`frame_index mod buf_size`). Because the record is now a straight overwrite
(`tape = Send * input`, WRITE-RECORD-MATH), the slot ends up holding whichever
Write drew last (last-writer-wins) at that Write's own Send and input; the outcome
depends on draw order and is not a supported gesture in v3.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Deterministic multi-writer accumulation (N Writes summing into one slot) is the
deferred additive-IFS feature (DEFER-IFS). The barrier that would make it
deterministic exists but is not exercised here; this requirement makes the
current degenerate behaviour explicit rather than silently wrong. v0.3 note: with
the fade+additive record replaced by a straight overwrite, the degenerate result
is a draw-order-dependent last-writer-wins rather than the old compounding
decay-and-add — still undefined for the user, but simpler.
<<<
VERIFICATION: >>>
`dc_frame_tick` returns 0 (no advance) for the 2nd+ touch of a `frame_id`
(`delay-core/src/lib.rs`), but `draw_write` unconditionally runs its single
`write_pass` every frame (`delay-write/src/lib.rs`), so two Writes on one Channel
each overwrite the same slot in draw order and the last one wins.
<<<
STATUS: Draft

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
# Non-goals (deferred to fast-follow)
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DEFER-MULTITAP
TITLE: Variable and multi-tap
COMPONENT: Tap
STATEMENT: >>>
Variable tap offset (0..loop) and K combined taps are OUT OF SCOPE for v3. The
Tap read position is fixed at full delay.
<<<
STATUS: Deferred

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DEFER-FLOAT
TITLE: Over-unity persistence and the resolution↔depth budget
COMPONENT: Core
STATEMENT: >>>
The precision half of the original DEFER-FLOAT has LANDED: the tape is now RGBA16F
(CORE-FORMAT) stored at reduced resolution (WRITE-TAPE-SCALE), removing feedback-
tail banding at ~half the old VRAM. What remains OUT OF SCOPE for v3:
(a) over-unity (>1.0) *persistence* across laps — blocked by the host's 8-bit
inter-effect FBO in the loop and the overwrite-Write; needs host float compositing
and/or additive accumulation (DEFER-IFS); and (b) trading tape resolution for a
deeper buffer / longer max delay (a dynamic `BUFFER_DEPTH` driven by the scale) —
the tape scale is currently a fixed 0.5×, and a live Crisp<->Deep control is a
possible follow-up.
<<<
STATUS: Deferred

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DEFER-SWEEP
TITLE: Sweepable continuous time
COMPONENT: Write
STATEMENT: >>>
Continuous/interpolated time sweeps are OUT OF SCOPE for v3. Time is latched
beat-sync / ms / frames.
<<<
STATUS: Deferred

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DEFER-PALETTE
TITLE: Raw palette controls
COMPONENT: Core
STATEMENT: >>>
Wrap mode (clamp/repeat/mirror), filter (nearest/linear), and no-clear-on-
realloc are OUT OF SCOPE for v3.
<<<
STATUS: Deferred

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DEFER-IFS
TITLE: Additive multi-writer IFS accumulation
COMPONENT: Core
STATEMENT: >>>
N Writes summing deterministically into one slot per frame (contractive IFS
attractors, absorbing the old overdub feature) is OUT OF SCOPE for v3. v3 is
single-writer-per-channel; the barrier's multi-writer determinism is present but
unexercised.
<<<
STATUS: Deferred

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DEFER-CHANNELS
TITLE: Channel count 4–8
COMPONENT: Core
STATEMENT: >>>
Increasing `NUM_CHANNELS` beyond 2 is OUT OF SCOPE for v3 (VRAM-heavy).
<<<
STATUS: Deferred

